        ":question/id": "35cc2bef-7c45-5174-b681-8a4353622923",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-40908",
        ":question/problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})-2?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and then subtracting $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\  & -4\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 4 \\\\  & -6\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}(x+{30}^{∘})-2\\le 2 \\\\  & -6\\le f(x)\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-6,2].$"
      },
